# Thai Male Fruity-Woody Market Brief — SUKHUM 35

**Research freeze:** 2026-08-29, Asia/Bangkok  
**Scope:** Thai men buying Thai perfume for themselves; foreign tourists and export buyers are outside the target.  
**Claim mode:** Current-source market research plus clearly labeled design hypotheses. No sales forecast, sensory result, or consumer acceptance is proven by this document.

## Observed market facts

Thailand's Trade Policy and Strategy Office, citing Euromonitor, reported a Thai perfume market value of USD 391 million in 2024 and forecast USD 491 million in 2028, a 5.86% compound annual growth rate. The same government article argues that Thai fragrance brands can compete through distinctive identity, local aromatic materials, standards, e-commerce, social media, and workshops. This supports category opportunity, but it does not prove demand for this particular formula or price. [TPSO perfume-market article](https://tpso.go.th/news/2502-0000000031)

GQ Thailand's February 2026 men's-fragrance roundup describes Thai-brand men's perfume as moving from niche use into a broader, easier-to-buy category. Its selected products emphasize daily cleanliness, soft warmth, dimensionality, understated masculinity, and composed or trustworthy character. Prices in that editorial range from THB 1,830 for 60 mL to THB 9,900 for 100 mL, illustrating that local-brand positioning spans accessible niche through luxury rather than one uniform tier. [GQ Thailand, 1 February 2026](https://gqthailand.com/article/5-sophisticated-thai-brand-men-perfumes)

Thai Meteorological Department guidance reports annual relative humidity around 72–74% in the central region, falling to roughly 62–69% in summer and rising to about 79% in the rainy period. That climate makes heat-accelerated top-note loss, indoor/outdoor transitions, and perceived heaviness important design questions; it does not itself predict a perfume's measured longevity or projection. [Thai Meteorological Department humidity reference](https://www5.tmd.go.th/info/%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%98)

## Direct competitive reference

MITH Mystery for Him is the clearest local comparator for an Aventus-family commercial space. The official page lists perfumer Jean-Christophe Hérault; lemon and pink pepper in the top; jasmine and juniper berry in the middle; vanilla, patchouli, oakmoss, and amber in the base; sizes of 15, 60, and 100 mL; and a current displayed range of THB 699–2,859. MITH itself describes the profile as bright, juicy fruity, spicy, slightly smoky, softly sweet, ambered, musky, and skin-like. [MITH Mystery for Him official product page](https://mithbangkok.com/product/mystery-for-him/)

This means that a generic smoother/sweeter Aventus-style product is not open whitespace in Thailand. SUKHUM 35 should compete through a different balance: less vanilla/amber sweetness, less juniper-green lift, a more adult dry cedar/vetiver body, a warmer red-mandarin entry, and a narrow charcoal seam rather than a broad smoky or mossy base. That conclusion is an inference from the comparator architecture, not a measured consumer preference.

## International price anchors

At the research freeze, the Thai Central listing for Creed Aventus 100 mL showed approximately THB 13,800, while the Montblanc fragrance listing showed Explorer 100 mL around THB 4,680 promotional / THB 5,200 regular. Retail prices can change and should be rechecked immediately before commercial pricing. [Central Creed Aventus listing](https://www.central.co.th/th/creed-aventus-perfume-edp-100-ml-cds15410238) and [Central Montblanc fragrance listing](https://www.central.co.th/th/montblanc/beauty/fragrance)

These anchors leave a plausible local-brand corridor beneath designer pricing and above low-cost counter-brand decants. A **working hypothesis**, not a recommendation backed by willingness-to-pay data, is THB 390–490 for 10 mL and THB 1,590–1,890 for 50 mL. The price hypothesis must be tested against actual formula cost, packaging, notification, channel margin, repeat purchase, and blind preference.

## Regulatory boundary

Thai FDA classifies perfume as a cosmetic. Its entrepreneur guidance states that manufacturing/import facilities and cosmetic notification requirements apply, and that cosmetic labels must be clearly readable in Thai. The Cosmetics Act also requires notified products to conform to their notification information before sale. This prototype cannot be represented as commercially releasable merely because the formula pipeline passes. [Thai FDA cosmetic definition](https://en.fda.moph.go.th/entrepreneurs-cosmetics/what-are-cosmetic-01/), [Thai FDA permission and Thai-label guidance](https://en.fda.moph.go.th/entrepreneurs-cosmetics/category/how-to-apply-for-permission-on-cosmetics/), and [Cosmetics Act B.E. 2558 English PDF](https://catalog.fda.moph.go.th/dataset/1e51956a-5718-4aa9-8b23-076349ed1856/resource/848bf9fe-ee17-4d7d-89e3-f4d6e304e6eb/download/cosmetics-act-b.e.-2015.pdf)

Before sale, a Thai regulatory professional should verify the current notification, ingredient, restricted-material, warning, manufacturing, stability, packaging, batch, and label requirements against the actual supplier documents and finished product. This dossier is not legal or safety approval.

## Target consumer hypothesis

- Thai man, approximately 22–40.
- Buys fragrance for his own office, transit, casual-date, and evening use.
- Wants recognizable masculine polish and performance without a loud sweet-blue or vanilla-amber identity.
- Values a Thai brand and a composed, trustworthy image more than an exact foreign-clone claim.
- Likely encounters outdoor heat and humidity followed by strong indoor air conditioning.

Every point above is a segment hypothesis to test, not an observed demographic fact about all Thai men.

## Product position

**Name:** สุขุม 35 / SUKHUM 35  
**Positioning line:** Composed fruit, charcoal, and dry Bangkok woods.  
**Olfactive family:** Modern fruity-smoky transparent woods.  
**Recognition strategy:** Familiar citrus/pineapple/cassis entry, distinct dry Thai-market body.  
**First format:** 30 mL controlled prototype at 18% nominal product-stock concentrate; commercial formats remain undecided.

The product should remain recognizably Aventus-adjacent in the first hour but must not be sold or documented as Creed, an authenticated reconstruction, or an exact clone. The differentiating signature is the red-mandarin/grapefruit lens, secondary cassis, Clearwood/cedar/vetiver body, restrained Cade charcoal, trace Siam-benzoin warmth, and sole Romandolide projection layer.

## Formula implications

1. Keep pineapple and cassis legible through the early heart, but suppress candy, cooked-fruit, and catty-green excess.
2. Keep smoke subordinate and clean; omit Birch Tar, heavy Nagarmortha, oakmoss, and leather in V1.
3. Use a dry wood object rather than a sweet amber bed: Iso E Super, Clearwood, Virginia cedar, vetiver, and current homogeneous Ambrox Super.
4. Use no vanilla, ethyl maltol, coumarin, tonka, Ambermax, or Amber Xtreme in V1.
5. Use Romandolide as the single projection musk; do not build a clone-style musk chord.
6. Model Bangkok temperature and OAV-per-time as diagnostic stressors, then require real blinded wear testing.

## Commercial evidence still missing

- Blind liking and recognition results from the intended Thai male segment.
- Preference versus MITH Mystery for Him, Montblanc Explorer, and a verified Aventus sample.
- Measured projection, persistence, and stability in Bangkok conditions.
- Finished cost of goods, packaging, channel margin, and price elasticity.
- Supplier-specific IFRA certificates, allergen declarations, SDS/CoA/lot identity, and finished-product regulatory review.
- Repeat-purchase and return-rate evidence.

Until those are obtained, SUKHUM 35 is a market-informed formulation hypothesis, not a proven bestseller.
